Q: Rescale SVG to a Target size A: <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> You can easily use your current SVG e.g. for a 64x64px target size by applying CSS width/height properties or SVG presentation attributes.<br />
No need to recalculate the intrinsic coordinates.</p>
<h3>SVG assets/icons with explicit dimensions</h3>
<p>Most graphic editors automatically export SVGs with width and height attributes – the original output was probably something like this</p>
<pre><code>&lt;svg width=&quot;1000&quot; height=&quot;1000&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 1000 1000&quot;&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>Changing <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> to 64 would do the trick.<br />
<strong>Heads up!</strong> explicit dimensions can be inconvenient for usage in responsive layouts. Omitting dimension attributes in the SVG is usually preferable to allow for relative resizing (e.g relative to font-size or grid column width).</p>
<h3>Scaling SVG is usually not necessary</h3>
<p>The main benefit of scalable vector graphics – the ultimate display size is <em>mostly</em> irrelevant.<br />
Recalculating the actual SVG command coordinates to a target size also usually doesn't have significant benefits regarding file size.</p>
<p>However there are exceptions:</p>
<h4>When scaling SVG coordinates makes sense</h4>
<p>your native command coordinate space is extremely</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>large:</strong> CAD applications tend to store/export vector coordinates in real-life physical units with an &quot;insane&quot; precision. This precision makes sense for applications like CNC/Laser-plotting or 3D printing devices but are an overkill for graphic presentation</li>
<li>or <strong>small:</strong> GeoJSON data is based on physical latitudes and longitudes. When we retrieve polygon vertices for a city or smaller region we're often dealing with boundaries smaller than 1 user unit – so 3, 4 or more decimals are required. Sometimes we may even scratch the <a href="https://oreillymedia.github.io/Using_SVG/extras/ch08-precision.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">numerical limits of browser renderers</a></li>
</ul>
<h3>Common practices for SVG icon assets</h3>
<ul>
<li>FontAwesome uses an artboard size of up to 512x512 pixels, which allows for the use of integer coordinate values.</li>
<li>Material, Feather or Heroicons icons (and many other libraries) prefer a 24x24 reference size. This rather small size requires more attention to the required floating point accuracy as some more complex shapes simply can't be represented with integer only SVG command coordinates without introducing <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/75703312/how-to-reduce-quality-or-improve-rendering-performance-of-svg-file">ugly distortions</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Should I prefer the common 24x24px grid?</h4>
<p>The main reason many icon design libraries have opted for a 24x24 reference frame is the fact that a 24 grid is quite convenient (divisible by 2, 3, 4, 6) and 24px also happens to be a reasonably readable display size for UI elements.</p>
<p>Most importantly, if you &quot;abide&quot; to a 24px display size and all important contours are aligned to this 24x24 grid – there is a good chance the ultimate rendering is as crisp as it gets (so minimizing anti-aliasing artifacts which may make your rendering appear slightly blurry).</p>
<p>So it is definitely worth considering adopting this popular concept, as it enables you to mix existing library icons with your own custom ones. However, it is by no means a »silver-bullet« to a custom grid or artboard size and it won't ensure your SVG graphics will always render perfectly crisp!</p>
<h3>The perfect <code>viewBox</code>?</h3>
<p>Your example shows a typical icon usage - quite likely designed in Adobe Illustrator.
The icon is based on a 1000x1000 <code>viewBox</code> or reference frame – in a graphic editor this would be your artboard/document size.</p>
<p>A 1000x1000 <code>viewBox</code> usually provides enough space to use integer coordinates – <strong>trivia:</strong> CFF OpenType fonts traditionally used this coordinate space recommendation (1000 UPM - units per EM square) for glyphs/character shapes – sufficient even for smaller curvatures and details.</p>
<p>But as commented by <em>enxaneta</em> you may consider to decrease the white space around the current icon for a tighter bounding box.</p>
<p>However, a certain amount of white space is usually desired to avoid icons to bump into adjacent text-elements (such as labels).</p>
<h4>Consistent design concepts and grids</h4>
<p>Ideally, you should avoid mixing different design grids or icon libraries simply because it complicates the coding process e.g when applying stroke widths.</p>
<p>For instance it becomes quite inconvenient to normalize stroke widths across multiple different viewBox sizes:</p>
<ul>
<li>icons based on a 24x24 unit grid may work best with <code>stroke-width:2px</code></li>
<li>when based on a 1000x1000 space you probably need a &quot;clunkier&quot; CSS value like <code>16.47px</code> – albeit you may also circumvent this via CSS <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/vector-effect" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke</code></a> as commented by <em>Kaiido</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Keep as it is: »[...] without editing all the coordinates«?</h3>
<p>As previously explained, the native coordinate space shouldn't be the problem to get the desired rendering...</p>
<p>However, you most likely encounter issues when using SVGs directly as exported by graphic applications as they:</p>
<ul>
<li>usually have a lot of <strong>overhead</strong> e.g meta-data are not great in terms of file-size: useless for web applications</li>
<li><strong>unnecessary style</strong> properties - e.g for default values like <code>fill:black</code> that could be omitted</li>
<li><strong>overly specific style</strong> properties in form of inline styles which makes overriding these contextually unnecessarily hard</li>
<li>depending on your SVG embedding method: the generic Adobe Illustrator CSS class names can be problematic when inlining multiple assets to the DOM: a subsequent <code>.cls-1</code> will override previous <strong>global CSS</strong> rules with the same selector</li>
</ul>
<p>Here's an example of an &quot;optimized&quot; SVG</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body&gt;svg {
  display: block;
  outline: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: visible;
}

.icn-wrp {
  font-size: 64px;
  color: red;
}

.icn-svg {
  display: inline-block;
  height: 1em;
  transform: translateY(0.2em)
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;p class="icn-wrp"&gt;
  &lt;svg class="icn-svg" viewBox="0 0 1000 1000"&gt;&lt;use href="#Language" /&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;
  Language
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p class="icn-wrp" style="color:green"&gt;
